                <td width=572 valign=center colspan=10 style="width:343.6000pt;padding:0.0000pt 5.4000pt 0.0000pt 5.4000pt ;border-left:none;
mso-border-left-alt:none;border-right:1.0000pt solid windowtext;mso-border-right-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;
border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;border-bottom:1.0000pt solid windowtext;
mso-border-bottom-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;">
                    <p class=MsoNormal><span style="font-family:仿宋;mso-bidi-font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:12.0000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><font face="仿宋"><div>
<div>Medical Microbiology is a compulsory basic course for majors such as Clinical Medicine, Forensic Medicine and Nursing. It mainly consists of five parts: basic bacteriology, clinical bacteriology, basic virology, clinical virology and pathogenic fungi.</div>
</div>
<div>
<div>Basic bacteriology and basic virology mainly expound the morphological structure, growth and reproduction, heredity and variation, pathogenicity and immunity, laboratory diagnosis as well as specific prevention and treatment of bacteria and viruses.</div>
</div>
<div>
<div>Clinical bacteriology and clinical virology focus on describing the biological characteristics, pathogenicity and immune responses, microbiological examination and prevention and treatment of medically relevant pathogenic bacteria and viruses.</div>
</div>
<div>
<div>The part of pathogenic fungi mainly discusses the main characteristics of fungi and their relationships with medicine.</div>
</div></font></span><span style="font-family:'Times New Roman';mso-fareast-font-family:仿宋;font-size:10.5000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><o:p></o:p></span></p>
                </td>
            </tr>
